@page "/components/overlay"

<PageOutlet Url="components/overlay"
            Title="Overlay"
            Description="Overlay is the layer that covers the page - or a container of it - to catch its clicks and host a loader or a message over it." />

<DemoPage Name="Overlay"
          SecondaryNames="@(["Backdrop", "Scrim"])"
          Description="Overlay covers the page - or, positioned absolutely, one container of it - and hosts whatever is placed in it over everything else: a loader, a message, a surface of your own. A click on the layer or Escape dismisses it unless it is blocking, it can dim what it covers, place its content, hold the scroller behind it still, and be driven by binding or by methods."
          Parameters="componentParameters"
          PublicMembers="componentPublicMembers"
          SubEnums="componentSubEnums"
          CssVariables="componentCssVariables"
          GitHubUrl="Utilities/Overlay/BitOverlay.razor"
          GitHubDemoUrl="Utilities/Overlay/BitOverlayDemo.razor">
    <NotesTemplate>
        <BitText>
            The Overlay is a bare layer: it has no role and does not move the focus or trap it. For a dialog - a
            surface that takes the keyboard over until it is dealt with - use
            <BitLink Href="/components/modal">BitModal</BitLink>. The layer stops the pointer only, so make what it
            covers <b>inert</b> while it is open to keep the keyboard out as well; for a loader, also mark what is
            loading <b>aria-busy</b> and name the loader, as the AbsolutePosition example does.
        </BitText>
    </NotesTemplate>
    <Examples>
        <DemoExample Title="Basic" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example1CsharpCode" Id="example1">
            <div>
                <b>IsOpen</b> shows the Overlay and is two-way bound, so a click on the layer or Escape closes it. The
                layer is transparent unless <b>ModeFull</b> dims what it covers with the theme's overlay color, and it
                fades in and out (instantly under reduced motion). <b>Position</b> places the content; without it the
                content stretches over the whole layer.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="btn-container">
                <BitButton OnClick="() => basicIsOpen = true">Show Overlay</BitButton>
                <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="() => modeFullIsOpen = true">Show dimmed Overlay</BitButton>
            </div>
            <BitOverlay @bind-IsOpen="basicIsOpen" Position="BitPosition.Center">
                <BitProgress Circular Indeterminate Thickness="10" AriaLabel="Loading" />
            </BitOverlay>
            <BitOverlay @bind-IsOpen="modeFullIsOpen" Position="BitPosition.Center" ModeFull>
                <BitProgress Circular Indeterminate Thickness="10" AriaLabel="Loading" />
            </BitOverlay>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Position" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example2CsharpCode" Id="example2">
            <div>
                Nine places on the layer. The <b>Start</b> / <b>End</b> members follow the writing direction, the
                <b>Left</b> / <b>Right</b> ones stay on their side.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="position-grid">
                @foreach (var position in positions)
                {
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="() => OpenAt(position)">@position</BitButton>
                }
            </div>
            <BitOverlay @bind-IsOpen="positionIsOpen" Position="position" ModeFull>
                <div class="content small">@position</div>
            </BitOverlay>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Dismissal" RazorCode="@example3RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example3CsharpCode" Id="example3">
            <div>
                Only the layer dismisses the Overlay: a click on its content never does, nor does a text selection
                dragged out of it. Escape is the keyboard's equivalent of that click, wherever the focus is - an open
                dropdown or a dialog above takes the key first. <b>NoDismissOnEscape</b> keeps the key from closing
                it; <b>Blocking</b> keeps both from closing it, so leave a way out in the content.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="btn-container">
                <BitCheckbox Label="Blocking" @bind-Value="dismissalBlocking" />
                <BitCheckbox Label="NoDismissOnEscape" @bind-Value="dismissalNoEscape" />
                <BitButton OnClick="() => dismissalIsOpen = true">Show Overlay</BitButton>
            </div>
            <BitOverlay @bind-IsOpen="dismissalIsOpen"
                        Position="BitPosition.Center"
                        ModeFull
                        Blocking="dismissalBlocking"
                        NoDismissOnEscape="dismissalNoEscape">
                <div class="content">
                    <h3>Try to close me</h3>
                    <div>
                        Click the dimmed layer, press Escape, or select this text and release outside the box.
                        Clicks in here are the content's own.
                    </div>
                    <br />
                    <div class="btn-container">
                        <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="() => dismissalClicks++">Clicked @dismissalClicks time(s)</BitButton>
                        <BitButton OnClick="() => dismissalIsOpen = false">Close</BitButton>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </BitOverlay>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="AbsolutePosition" RazorCode="@example4RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example4CsharpCode" Id="example4">
            <div>
                <b>AbsolutePosition</b> covers the container the Overlay is declared in rather than the screen; the
                container needs <b>position: relative</b>, and its rounded corners carry over to the layer. The layer
                only stops the pointer: <b>inert</b> on what it covers keeps Tab out too, and <b>aria-busy</b> plus a
                named loader tell a screen reader what is going on.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitButton OnClick="LoadReport">Load</BitButton>
            <br /><br />
            <div class="container" aria-busy="@(absoluteIsOpen ? "true" : "false")">
                <div class="report" inert="@absoluteIsOpen">
                    <h3>Report</h3>
                    <BitTextField Label="Filter" />
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline">Export</BitButton>
                </div>
                <BitOverlay @bind-IsOpen="absoluteIsOpen" Position="BitPosition.Center" ModeFull AbsolutePosition Blocking>
                    <BitProgress Circular Indeterminate Thickness="10" AriaLabel="Loading the report" />
                </BitOverlay>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Scroll lock" RazorCode="@example5RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example5CsharpCode" Id="example5">
            <div>
                <b>AutoToggleScroll</b> stops the scroller behind the Overlay while it is open, without a layout shift,
                and hands it back once the last Overlay holding it closes. The scroller is the page by default, the one
                of the <b>BitAppShell</b> the Overlay is in, or the one <b>ScrollerSelector</b> (or
                <b>ScrollerElement</b>) names - the green box below. An Overlay left scrolling hands the wheel and
                the touch drag on to that scroller.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="btn-container">
                <BitButton OnClick="() => pageLockIsOpen = true">Lock the page</BitButton>
                <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="() => boxScrollIsOpen = true">Cover the box, keep it scrolling</BitButton>
                <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="() => boxLockIsOpen = true">Cover the box, lock it</BitButton>
            </div>
            <BitOverlay @bind-IsOpen="pageLockIsOpen" Position="BitPosition.Center" ModeFull AutoToggleScroll>
                <div class="content small">Try to scroll the page.</div>
            </BitOverlay>
            <div class="scroller">
                <BitOverlay @bind-IsOpen="boxScrollIsOpen"
                            Position="BitPosition.Center"
                            ScrollerSelector=".scroller"
                            AbsolutePosition>
                    <div class="content small">The box still scrolls behind me.</div>
                </BitOverlay>
                <BitOverlay @bind-IsOpen="boxLockIsOpen"
                            Position="BitPosition.Center"
                            ModeFull
                            ScrollerSelector=".scroller"
                            AbsolutePosition
                            AutoToggleScroll>
                    <div class="content small">The box is locked.</div>
                </BitOverlay>
                @for (int i = 1; i <= 20; i++)
                {
                    <div>Line @i of the scrolling box.</div>
                }
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Events" RazorCode="@example6RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example6CsharpCode" Id="example6">
            <div>
                <b>OnClick</b> reports every click on an open Overlay - on its content too, and the ones a Blocking
                Overlay refuses - so it can close one on your own terms, here on the third click. <b>OnOpen</b> and
                <b>OnClose</b> report the state however it changed: a click, Escape, the binding or a method.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitButton OnClick="() => eventsIsOpen = true">Show Overlay</BitButton>
            <BitOverlay @bind-IsOpen="eventsIsOpen"
                        Position="BitPosition.Center"
                        ModeFull
                        Blocking
                        OnClick="HandleOverlayClick"
                        OnOpen="HandleOverlayOpen"
                        OnClose="HandleOverlayClose">
                <div class="content small">Clicked @eventsClicks time(s). Closes on the third click.</div>
            </BitOverlay>
            <br /><br />
            <div>Opened @eventsOpened time(s), closed @eventsClosed time(s).</div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Programmatic control" RazorCode="@example7RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example7CsharpCode" Id="example7">
            <div>
                Without a binding, drive the Overlay through <b>Open</b>, <b>Close</b> and <b>Toggle</b> on its
                <b>@@ref</b>. <b>DefaultIsOpen</b> sets the state such an uncontrolled Overlay starts in.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitButton OnClick="OpenAndCloseLater">Open for 3 seconds</BitButton>
            <BitOverlay @ref="overlayRef" Position="BitPosition.Center" ModeFull>
                <div class="content small">Opened by Open() and closed by Close() - or by a click, or Escape.</div>
            </BitOverlay>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Cascading parameters" RazorCode="@example8RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example8CsharpCode" Id="example8">
            <div>
                <b>BitParams</b> hands a <b>BitOverlayParams</b> to every Overlay under it. The values are defaults: an
                Overlay keeps whatever it sets itself. The open state, the content, <b>ScrollerElement</b> and the
                callbacks stay per instance.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="btn-container">
                <BitButton OnClick="() => cascadedIsOpen = true">Takes the cascade</BitButton>
                <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="() => cascadedOwnIsOpen = true">Its own position</BitButton>
            </div>
            <BitParams Parameters="overlayParams">
                <BitOverlay @bind-IsOpen="cascadedIsOpen">
                    <div class="content small">Dimmed and centered - both from BitParams.</div>
                </BitOverlay>
                <BitOverlay @bind-IsOpen="cascadedOwnIsOpen" Position="BitPosition.TopCenter">
                    <div class="content small">My own Position, the rest from BitParams.</div>
                </BitOverlay>
            </BitParams>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Style & Class" RazorCode="@example9RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example9CsharpCode" Id="example9">
            <div>
                <b>Style</b> and <b>Class</b> land on the layer itself. The public <b>--bit-Overlay-*</b> CSS variables
                inherit, so one set on <b>:root</b> restyles every Overlay and one set on an Overlay's <b>Style</b>
                restyles that one and any Overlay nested in it: here a tinted, blurred, slower-fading layer with padding
                around a bottom-placed message.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="btn-container">
                <BitButton OnClick="() => styleIsOpen = true">Style</BitButton>
                <BitButton OnClick="() => classIsOpen = true">Class</BitButton>
                <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="() => cssVarsIsOpen = true">CSS variables</BitButton>
            </div>
            <BitOverlay @bind-IsOpen="styleIsOpen"
                        Position="BitPosition.Center"
                        Style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(78, 0, 142, 0.55), rgba(255, 0, 96, 0.35));">
                <BitProgress Circular Indeterminate Thickness="10" AriaLabel="Loading" />
            </BitOverlay>
            <div>
                <BitOverlay @bind-IsOpen="classIsOpen" Position="BitPosition.Center" Class="custom-overlay">
                    <BitProgress Circular Indeterminate Thickness="10" AriaLabel="Loading" />
                </BitOverlay>
            </div>
            <BitOverlay @bind-IsOpen="cssVarsIsOpen"
                        ModeFull
                        Position="BitPosition.BottomCenter"
                        Style="--bit-Overlay-background: #1e1b4b99; --bit-Overlay-backdrop-filter: blur(4px); --bit-Overlay-padding: 2rem; --bit-Overlay-transition-duration: 400ms;">
                <div class="content small">Tint, blur, padding and a slower fade all come from variables.</div>
            </BitOverlay>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="RTL" RazorCode="@example10RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example10CsharpCode" Id="example10">
            <div>
                <b>Dir="BitDir.Rtl"</b> lays the content out right-to-left, and the <b>Start</b> / <b>End</b>
                positions follow it.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div dir="rtl">
                <BitButton Dir="BitDir.Rtl" OnClick="() => rtlIsOpen = true">نمایش روکش</BitButton>
            </div>
            <BitOverlay @bind-IsOpen="rtlIsOpen" Dir="BitDir.Rtl" Position="BitPosition.TopStart" ModeFull>
                <div class="content small">
                    روزی روزگاری، داستان‌ها میان مردم پیوند می‌ساختند؛ هم‌نوایی صداهایی که رویاهای مشترک می‌آفریدند.
                </div>
            </BitOverlay>
        </DemoExample>
    </Examples>
</DemoPage>
